    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Which laryngeal cartilage is the only one that forms a complete, unyielding ring around the airway?', '["Thyroid cartilage", "Cricoid cartilage", "Arytenoid cartilage", "Corniculate cartilage", "Epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cricoid cartilage is the only complete cartilaginous ring in the respiratory tract. It is narrowest in the pediatric airway and prone to causing subglottic stenosis if traumatized (e.g., by prolonged intubation).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary function of the posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA) muscle?', '["Adduction of the vocal folds", "Abduction of the vocal folds", "Lengthening of the vocal folds", "Shortening of the vocal folds", "Depression of the epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The posterior cricoarytenoid muscle is the sole abductor (opener) of the true vocal folds. It is innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which intrinsic laryngeal muscle is NOT innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN)?', '["Thyroarytenoid", "Lateral cricoarytenoid", "Posterior cricoarytenoid", "Cricothyroid", "Transverse arytenoid"]'::jsonb, 3, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles are innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerve EXCEPT the cricothyroid muscle, which is innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve (SLN).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The superficial lamina propria of the true vocal fold is also known as:', '["The vocalis muscle", "Reinke''s space", "The vocal ligament", "The conus elasticus", "The quadrangular membrane"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The superficial lamina propria is known as Reinke''s space. It is a loose, gelatinous matrix that vibrates during phonation. Fluid accumulation here causes Reinke''s edema, and vessel rupture causes vocal fold hemorrhage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following spaces is bounded superiorly by the false vocal folds and inferiorly by the true vocal folds?', '["Supraglottis", "Laryngeal ventricle", "Subglottis", "Vallecula", "Pyriform sinus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The laryngeal ventricle is the space between the true and false vocal folds. It contains mucous glands that lubricate the true vocal folds.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve (SLN) pierces which membrane to provide sensory innervation to the supraglottis?', '["Cricothyroid membrane", "Thyrohyoid membrane", "Quadrangular membrane", "Conus elasticus", "Cricotracheal membrane"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The internal branch of the SLN pierces the thyrohyoid membrane (along with the superior laryngeal artery) to provide sensory innervation to the larynx above the glottis. This is the nerve blocked during an awake intubation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a thyroidectomy, a surgeon inadvertently ligates the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. What specific voice deficit will the patient most likely experience?', '["A weak, breathy voice with aspiration", "Inability to sing high pitches", "Severe biphasic stridor", "Complete aphonia", "A harsh, barking cough"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The external branch of the SLN innervates the cricothyroid muscle, which elongates and tenses the vocal folds to elevate pitch. Injury causes loss of upper vocal range and vocal fatigue.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The right recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around which vascular structure?', '["Aortic arch", "Right subclavian artery", "Right common carotid artery", "Brachiocephalic trunk", "Right pulmonary artery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The right RLN loops around the right subclavian artery, while the left RLN loops around the aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum), giving the left RLN a much longer intrathoracic course and making it more susceptible to injury from thoracic pathology (e.g., lung cancer, aortic aneurysms).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The vocal ligament is formed by the thickened upper edge of which structure?', '["Quadrangular membrane", "Thyrohyoid membrane", "Conus elasticus", "Cricothyroid membrane", "Aryepiglottic fold"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The vocal ligament is the thickened superior edge of the conus elasticus (cricovocal membrane). It forms the intermediate and deep layers of the lamina propria.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which intrinsic muscle of the larynx is primarily responsible for relaxing and shortening the vocal folds (lowering pitch)?', '["Cricothyroid", "Posterior cricoarytenoid", "Thyroarytenoid", "Lateral cricoarytenoid", "Interarytenoid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The thyroarytenoid (vocalis) muscle constitutes the main body of the vocal fold. Its contraction shortens and relaxes the vocal fold cover, lowering the pitch of the voice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which branchial arch gives rise to the thyroid cartilage?', '["First arch", "Second arch", "Third arch", "Fourth arch", "Sixth arch"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fourth branchial arch gives rise to the thyroid cartilage and the cricothyroid muscle. The sixth arch gives rise to the cricoid and arytenoid cartilages and the remaining intrinsic laryngeal muscles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The lymphatic drainage of the glottis (true vocal cords) is characterized by:', '["Rich, bilaterally crossing drainage to levels II and III", "Dense drainage to the pretracheal (Delphian) nodes", "Virtually no lymphatic drainage", "Drainage to the submandibular (Level I) nodes", "Drainage to the supraclavicular nodes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The true vocal folds (glottis) are unique in that they possess virtually no lymphatic drainage. This is why early glottic squamous cell carcinoma (T1, T2) very rarely presents with regional lymph node metastasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which structure separates the nasopharynx from the oropharynx?', '["Hard palate", "Soft palate", "Epiglottis", "Hyoid bone", "Vallecula"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The soft palate (velum) forms the boundary between the nasopharynx (above) and oropharynx (below).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a left recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis is found to have a mass in the aortopulmonary window. Which condition is most likely?', '["Thyroid nodule", "Pancoast tumor", "Apical lung tumor", "Metastatic lung cancer or mediastinal lymphoma", "Carotid body tumor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The left RLN has a long course descending into the thorax, looping beneath the aortic arch through the aortopulmonary window. Mass lesions here (lung cancer, lymphoma, aortic aneurysm) often cause left RLN palsy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which muscle is responsible for closing the posterior glottic chink by pulling the arytenoid cartilages together?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid", "Lateral cricoarytenoid", "Interarytenoid (transverse and oblique)", "Thyroarytenoid", "Cricothyroid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The interarytenoid muscles (transverse and oblique) are the only unpaired intrinsic muscles of the larynx. They adduct the arytenoid cartilages, closing the posterior portion of the glottis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male presents with hoarseness and a left vocal cord paralysis. A CT scan of the neck from the skull base to the clavicles is completely normal. What is the most appropriate next step in imaging?', '["MRI of the brain", "CT of the chest", "Ultrasound of the thyroid", "PET scan", "No further imaging is needed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Since the left RLN loops under the aortic arch in the chest, a CT of the neck will miss pathology in the upper mediastinum. A CT of the chest with contrast is mandatory to evaluate the entire course of the left RLN.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The adult larynx is located at approximately which vertebral levels?', '["C1-C3", "C3-C6", "C5-T1", "T1-T4", "C2-C4"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In adults, the larynx spans from approximately C3 to C6. In infants, it is located much higher (C1-C4), allowing them to breathe and swallow simultaneously.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which laryngeal cartilage is composed entirely of elastic cartilage?', '["Thyroid", "Cricoid", "Arytenoid", "Epiglottis", "Tracheal rings"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The epiglottis, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages are composed of elastic cartilage and do not ossify with age. The thyroid, cricoid, and arytenoid cartilages are hyaline cartilage and ossify significantly in adulthood.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most superficial layer of the true vocal fold in a healthy adult?', '["Stratified squamous epithelium", "Ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium", "Superficial lamina propria", "Vocalis muscle", "Conus elasticus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The true vocal folds are uniquely lined by stratified squamous epithelium to withstand the mechanical stress of phonation. The rest of the respiratory tract (including the false folds and subglottis) is lined by ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The space between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis is called the:', '["Pyriform sinus", "Laryngeal ventricle", "Vallecula", "Postcricoid space", "Glottis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The valleculae are the paired depressions between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis. A Macintosh laryngoscope blade is placed here during intubation to indirectly lift the epiglottis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common cause of congenital stridor in an infant?', '["Subglottic stenosis", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Laryngomalacia", "Tracheomalacia", "Laryngeal web"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Laryngomalacia is by far the most common cause of stridor in infants, accounting for 60-75% of cases. It is characterized by dynamic inspiratory collapse of floppy supraglottic structures.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-week-old infant presents with high-pitched inspiratory stridor that worsens when feeding, crying, or lying supine. The baby is gaining weight normally. What is the classic finding on awake flexible laryngoscopy?', '["Subglottic narrowing (steeple sign)", "Omega-shaped epiglottis with prolapsing aryepiglottic folds", "Bilateral vocal cord immobility", "Anterior glottic web", "A submucosal mass below the vocal cords"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Laryngomalacia is diagnosed clinically and confirmed by endoscopy showing an omega-shaped, retroflexed epiglottis and short, redundant aryepiglottic folds that suck inward during inspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the usual natural history of uncomplicated laryngomalacia?', '["It relentlessly progresses to airway obstruction", "It resolves spontaneously by 12-18 months of age", "It requires lifelong tracheostomy", "It causes permanent vocal cord paralysis", "It transforms into a laryngeal cyst"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Over 90% of laryngomalacia cases are self-limiting and resolve by 12-24 months as the laryngeal cartilages mature and stiffen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered an indication for surgical intervention (supraglottoplasty) in an infant with laryngomalacia?', '["Loud stridor that bothers the parents", "Stridor that worsens with crying", "Failure to thrive with poor weight gain and severe feeding difficulties", "Mild GERD", "Age greater than 6 months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Surgery (supraglottoplasty) is reserved for severe cases marked by failure to thrive, cor pulmonale, severe cyanotic episodes, or apneic events causing brain hypoxia. Mild/moderate cases are observed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which medical condition frequently exacerbates the symptoms of laryngomalacia and is empirically treated in most symptomatic infants?', '["Asthma", "Allergic rhinitis", "Laryngopharyngeal/gastroesophageal reflux (LPR/GERD)", "Hypothyroidism", "Cystic fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'GERD is highly prevalent in infants with laryngomalacia. The refluxate causes inflammation and edema of the already floppy supraglottic tissues, worsening the stridor. Anti-reflux therapy (PPI/H2 blocker) is standard.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a supraglottoplasty for severe laryngomalacia, what specific anatomic structures are surgically altered?', '["The true vocal folds are trimmed", "The cricoid cartilage is expanded", "The short, tight aryepiglottic folds are divided (lasered/snipped) to release the epiglottis", "The false vocal folds are removed", "The trachea is stented"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Supraglottoplasty involves dividing the tight aryepiglottic folds and optionally removing redundant supra-arytenoid mucosa to prevent them from prolapsing into the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An infant with inspiratory stridor is found to have laryngomalacia on flexible endoscopy. What additional diagnostic study is recommended to rule out synchronous airway lesions?', '["CT scan of the neck", "MRI of the brain", "Rigid bronchoscopy in the operating room", "Barium swallow", "pH probe study"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Up to 15-20% of infants with laryngomalacia have a synchronous airway lesion (e.g., subglottic stenosis, tracheomalacia). Rigid direct laryngoscopy and bronchoscopy (DLB) under general anesthesia allows evaluation of the entire airway below the cords.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Laryngomalacia produces stridor during which phase of respiration?', '["Inspiration only", "Expiration only", "Both inspiration and expiration (biphasic)", "Only during breath-holding", "Only during coughing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Laryngomalacia is a supraglottic lesion. The negative pressure generated during inspiration pulls the floppy tissues inward, causing inspiratory stridor.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 2-month-old presents with inspiratory stridor. The stridor is significantly louder when the infant is supine and almost disappears when the infant is prone. This positional change is classic for:', '["Subglottic hemangioma", "Tracheomalacia", "Laryngomalacia", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Laryngeal web"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Positional stridor (worse supine, better prone) is the hallmark of laryngomalacia. Gravity causes the epiglottis and supraglottic tissues to fall backward over the airway when supine.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An infant born with Pierre Robin sequence presents with severe inspiratory stridor. What is the primary cause of airway obstruction in this condition?', '["Laryngomalacia", "Glossoptosis (tongue falling backward) due to micrognathia", "Subglottic stenosis", "Choanal atresia", "Tracheoesophageal fistula"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pierre Robin sequence is characterized by micrognathia, glossoptosis, and a U-shaped cleft palate. The small jaw forces the tongue backward, obstructing the supraglottic airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the second most common cause of congenital stridor in neonates?', '["Laryngomalacia", "Subglottic stenosis", "Vocal fold paralysis", "Subglottic hemangioma", "Laryngeal web"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Vocal fold paralysis (unilateral or bilateral) is the second most common cause of congenital stridor. Unilateral is often iatrogenic (e.g., after PDA ligation), while bilateral is often idiopathic or associated with CNS anomalies (Arnold-Chiari).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A neonate presents with severe biphasic stridor and respiratory distress immediately after birth. The cry is surprisingly normal and strong. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Bilateral vocal fold paralysis", "Unilateral vocal fold paralysis", "Laryngomalacia", "Laryngeal web", "Laryngoesophageal cleft"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bilateral vocal fold paralysis typically presents with severe airway obstruction (biphasic stridor) because the cords rest near the midline, but the voice/cry is often normal because the cords are close enough to phonate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A neonate is born with a weak, breathy cry and mild feeding difficulties (choking on liquids), but has no respiratory distress. What is the most likely laryngeal diagnosis?', '["Bilateral vocal fold paralysis", "Unilateral vocal fold paralysis", "Subglottic stenosis", "Laryngomalacia", "Laryngeal web"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Unilateral vocal fold paralysis presents with a weak, breathy cry and aspiration risk due to an incompetent glottic valve, but the airway is adequate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 3-month-old infant presents with progressive, worsening biphasic stridor over the last 4 weeks. The infant has a large, red, raised ''strawberry'' lesion on their left cheek. What laryngeal finding is most likely?', '["Laryngomalacia", "Subglottic hemangioma", "Laryngeal web", "Vocal fold nodule", "Laryngeal papillomatosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Subglottic hemangiomas typically present at 2-6 months of age with progressive stridor as the hemangioma proliferates. Up to 50% of patients have associated cutaneous hemangiomas in the "beard" distribution.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the current first-line medical treatment for a symptomatic infantile subglottic hemangioma?', '["Systemic corticosteroids", "Oral Propranolol", "Vincristine", "Interferon alpha", "Immediate surgical excision"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oral propranolol (a non-selective beta-blocker) has revolutionized the treatment of infantile hemangiomas, causing rapid involution and avoiding the need for surgery or systemic steroids in most cases.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A newborn presents with a weak cry and biphasic stridor. Direct laryngoscopy reveals a thick, fibrous band connecting the anterior two-thirds of the true vocal folds. What is this anomaly called?', '["Subglottic stenosis", "Anterior glottic web", "Laryngocele", "Vocal fold nodule", "Reinke''s edema"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An anterior glottic web results from incomplete recanalization of the larynx during embryogenesis. It blunts the anterior commissure, causing hoarseness and variable airway obstruction.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A child with a congenital anterior glottic web is found to have a microdeletion on chromosome 22q11.2. Which genetic syndrome does this patient have?', '["Down syndrome", "Turner syndrome", "DiGeorge syndrome (Velocardiofacial syndrome)", "Treacher Collins syndrome", "Crouzon syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is a strong association between anterior glottic webs and 22q11.2 deletion syndrome (DiGeorge/Velocardiofacial syndrome). Genetic testing is recommended for any child presenting with a congenital web.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A newborn experiences repeated episodes of severe cyanosis and choking immediately upon attempting to feed, but breathes perfectly normally when crying. The nurse is unable to pass a nasogastric tube into the stomach. What is the diagnosis?', '["Laryngeal web", "Choanal atresia", "Laryngomalacia", "Tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF)", "Laryngeal cleft"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bilateral choanal atresia causes paradoxical cyanosis: the infant turns blue while quiet/feeding (because neonates are obligate nasal breathers) but turns pink when crying (which forces mouth breathing).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A newborn presents with recurrent aspiration pneumonia, cyanosis with feeding, and a weak cry. Direct laryngoscopy shows an abnormal communication between the posterior larynx and the anterior esophagus. What is the diagnosis?', '["Laryngeal web", "Laryngotracheoesophageal cleft", "Subglottic stenosis", "Laryngomalacia", "Tracheomalacia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A laryngeal cleft is a rare congenital anomaly caused by failure of the tracheoesophageal septum to fuse. It allows direct spillage of food and secretions into the airway, causing severe aspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which classification system is used to grade the severity of congenital laryngeal clefts based on how far inferiorly the cleft extends?', '["Cotton-Myer grading", "Benjamin-Inglis classification", "House-Brackmann scale", "Gleason score", "Mallampati score"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Benjamin-Inglis classification grades laryngeal clefts from Type 1 (supraglottic, above true cords) to Type 4 (extending deep into the thoracic trachea).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common cause of ACQUIRED subglottic stenosis in children?', '["Congenital malformation", "Prolonged endotracheal intubation", "Swallowing a button battery", "Gastroesophageal reflux", "Viral croup"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Prolonged endotracheal intubation in the neonatal ICU (e.g., for prematurity) is the leading cause of acquired subglottic stenosis. The cuff pressure causes ischemia and scarring of the cricoid ring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which anatomic feature of the pediatric airway makes it particularly susceptible to subglottic stenosis after intubation?', '["The vocal folds are easily torn", "The cricoid cartilage is the only complete cartilaginous ring in the airway and is the narrowest point in a child", "The epiglottis is floppy", "The trachea is too short", "The cartilage is fully ossified"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because the cricoid is a complete ring, any mucosal swelling or ischemic injury inside it cannot expand outward; it inevitably narrows the internal lumen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The severity of subglottic stenosis is commonly graded by estimating the percentage of luminal obstruction. What is this grading system called?', '["Benjamin-Inglis system", "Cotton-Myer grading system", "TNM staging", "Centor criteria", "Kocher criteria"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Cotton-Myer grading system evaluates the percentage of airway narrowing: Grade I (<50%), Grade II (51-70%), Grade III (71-99%), Grade IV (no detectable lumen).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 2-year-old child born prematurely at 25 weeks gestation was intubated for 2 months in the NICU. He now has recurrent episodes of ''croup'' that take weeks to resolve and requires frequent hospitalizations. What underlying diagnosis must be suspected?', '["Laryngomalacia", "Acquired subglottic stenosis", "Asthma", "Cystic fibrosis", "Vocal cord paralysis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Children with mild-to-moderate acquired subglottic stenosis often present with "recurrent croup." A mild viral URI causes a small amount of mucosal edema, which critically narrows their already compromised airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A child with a Cotton-Myer Grade III subglottic stenosis (90% obstruction) is tracheostomy dependent. What open surgical procedure is commonly performed to expand the cricoid ring and achieve decannulation?', '["Supraglottoplasty", "Laryngotracheal reconstruction (LTR) with cartilage grafting", "Total laryngectomy", "Tonsillectomy", "Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Laryngotracheal reconstruction (LTR) involves splitting the cricoid ring (anteriorly, posteriorly, or both) and inserting a piece of cartilage (usually from the rib or thyroid ala) to physically expand the airway lumen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which surgical procedure for severe subglottic stenosis involves completely resecting the scarred segment of the airway and sewing the healthy trachea directly to the cricoid or thyroid cartilage?', '["Laryngotracheal reconstruction (LTR)", "Cricotracheal resection (CTR)", "Tracheostomy", "Bronchoscopic balloon dilation", "Slide tracheoplasty"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cricotracheal resection (CTR) involves excising the stenotic anterior cricoid arch and upper tracheal rings, followed by primary end-to-end anastomosis. It has a higher decannulation rate for severe (Grade III/IV) stenosis than LTR.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-month-old infant with congenital subglottic stenosis presents with an elliptical, flattened cricoid ring shape rather than a circular one. What is the fundamental difference between congenital and acquired subglottic stenosis?', '["Congenital is caused by viral infection", "Congenital involves cartilaginous malformation of the cricoid, whereas acquired involves mucosal/submucosal fibrosis inside a normal cricoid", "Acquired cannot be treated with surgery", "Congenital always resolves spontaneously", "Acquired is associated with Down syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Congenital subglottic stenosis is a structural anomaly of the cartilage itself (e.g., an elliptical cricoid). Acquired stenosis is due to scar tissue forming inside a structurally normal ring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When evaluating a pediatric airway with rigid bronchoscopy, how does the surgeon estimate the size of the airway to determine the grade of subglottic stenosis?', '["Using a CT scan", "By measuring the largest outer diameter of an endotracheal tube that passes with a slight leak", "By visually guessing the diameter", "Using an MRI", "Using a ruler placed down the throat"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The airway diameter is objectively measured by "sizing." Endotracheal tubes of known outer diameters are passed until one fits snugly but allows a minimal air leak around it at 20 cm H2O pressure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-year-old child presents with chronic stridor and a history of repeated intubations. Endoscopy shows a thick web connecting the posterior aspects of the true vocal folds, limiting their abduction. What is this lesion called?', '["Anterior glottic web", "Posterior glottic synechia (web)", "Subglottic stenosis", "Vocal cord nodule", "Laryngocele"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A posterior glottic web (synechia) often results from mucosal ulceration over the arytenoids during intubation, which heals by scarring together, fixing the cords in a partially closed position.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What endoscopic intervention is frequently used as a first-line treatment for short-segment, soft, immature acquired subglottic stenosis before resorting to open surgery?', '["Total laryngectomy", "Rigid bronchoscopic balloon dilation", "Placement of a permanent metal stent", "Injection of botulinum toxin", "Tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Endoscopic balloon dilation, often combined with steroid injection or radial laser incisions, can stretch early, soft strictures. However, it is less effective for dense, mature, cartilaginous stenosis.');
